'''weedy'''<sup>1</sup> (wē′di), ''a.'' [< ''weed''<sup>1</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Having the character of a weed; weed-like.
{{smaller block|Some of them are clever in a way; rooted fools by nature, who bear a ''weedy'' little blossom of wit, and suppose themselves to flower all over, like rhododendrons in the season. {{right|''D. C. Murray'', Weaker Vessel, xiv.}}}}
'''2.''' Consisting of weeds.
{{smaller block|<poem>Her ''weedy'' trophies and herself
Fell in the weeping brook.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', Hamlet, iv. 7. 175.}}
<poem>Nettles, kix, and all the ''weedy'' nation.</poem>
{{right|''G. Fletcher'', Christ’s Triumph over Death.}}}}
'''3.''' Abounding with weeds. ''Irving.''
{{smaller block|When the grain is ''weedy'', we must reap high. {{right|''S. Judd'', Margaret, ii. 8.}}}}
'''4.''' Not of good blood; not of good strength or mettle; scraggy; hence, worthless, as for breeding or racing purposes: as, a ''weedy'' horse. [Slang.]
{{smaller block|Along the middle of the street the main business was horse-dealing, and a gypsy hostler would trot out a succession of the ''weediest'' old screws that ever kept out of the kennels. {{right|''Harper’s Mag.'', LXXVI. 625.}}}}

'''weedy'''<sup>2</sup> (wē′di), ''a.'' [< ''weed''<sup>2</sup> + ''-y''<sup>1</sup>.] Clad in weeds, or widows’ mourning. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|She was as ''weedy'' as in the early days of her mourning. {{right|''Dickens.''}}
A ''weedy'' woman came sweeping up to us. {{right|''Longfellow'', Journal, Oct. 16, 1848.}}}}

